  <p>More on what a whole bathroom costs: <a href="/en/costs/bathroom-renovation/">bathroom renovation costs</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="walk-in">Walk-in shower</h2>
  <p>A walk-in shower (<em>ducha a ras de suelo</em> or <em>ducha de obra</em>) has no step: the floor continues and slopes gently to a linear drain or gully. A glass screen keeps the water in.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pros:</strong> spacious and light, no edges where dirt collects, the bathroom looks bigger, and it stays safe to use as you get older.</li>
    <li><strong>Cons:</strong> the floor has to be laid with a fall and the drain needs room for it; good waterproofing under the tiles is essential. A walk-in shower that is too small without a door can put water on the bathroom floor.</li>
    <li><strong>Tip:</strong> allow at least 90 × 120 cm, preferably 100 × 140 cm. A long linear drain along the wall makes large tiles or microcement possible.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="rain">Rain shower</h2>
  <p>A rain shower (<em>ducha efecto lluvia</em> or <em>rociador</em>) is a large, flat shower head of 25 to 40 cm above your head. The water falls like gentle rain. It is not a separate type of shower but a head you choose for a walk-in shower or tray.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Surface-mounted set:</strong> a riser rail with rain head, hand shower and thermostatic mixer on the wall. Works in an existing shower.</li>
    <li><strong>Concealed:</strong> the valves sit inside the wall and the rain head comes from the ceiling or wall. Sleek, but the wall has to be opened.</li>
    <li><strong>Always combine it with a hand shower.</strong> It makes cleaning the shower easy and means you do not have to wet your hair every time.</li>
    <li><strong>Limescale:</strong> the water in the Axarquía is hard. Choose a head with rubber nozzles you can rub clean, and consider a water softener or scale filter.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="tray">Shower tray with glass screen</h2>
  <p>A shower tray (<em>plato de ducha</em>) in resin or mineral composite sits on the floor with a very low lip of 2 to 4 cm. Modern trays are flat, slip-resistant and available in any size.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pros:</strong> cheaper and quicker than a walk-in shower, fits where the drain has little room, and a new tray can often go where the old bath was.</li>
    <li><strong>Cons:</strong> a small lip, and the joint between tray and tiles must be sealed well.</li>
    <li><strong>Best for:</strong> apartments, budget renovations and lets.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="bath">Bath, shower bath or freestanding bath</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Built-in bath with shower screen:</strong> the classic combination. Handy with small children and if you like an occasional bath. Downside: stepping over the edge gets harder with age, and it takes up a lot of space.</li>
    <li><strong>Freestanding bath:</strong> the showpiece of a luxury bathroom or suite. Needs space around it and a floor drain. Uses the most water.</li>
    <li><strong>Shower and bath:</strong> if the bathroom is at least 7 to 8 m², a separate walk-in shower and a bath both fit. That is the best choice for the main bathroom of a villa.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="water">Water, pressure and boiler</h2>
  <p>In dry years the Axarquía sometimes has water restrictions, and on many fincas the water comes from a private tank or well. So think about water when you choose:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Consumption:</strong> a 5-minute shower with an eco head uses 30 to 45 litres, with a large rain shower 60 to 100 litres, a bath 150 to 300 litres.</li>
    <li><strong>Water pressure:</strong> a rain shower needs at least 2 to 3 bar. With low pressure you stand under a dripping plate.</li>
    <li><strong>Boiler:</strong> a rain shower or bath needs more hot water. A 50-litre water heater (<em>termo</em>) is then too small; choose 80 to 100 litres, or a heat-pump water heater that makes hot water on solar power.</li>
    <li><strong>Water tank:</strong> on a finca the tank decides how much you can shower.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="letting">For a B&B or holiday let</h2>
  <p>If you let to guests, the trade-offs differ from your own bathroom. Our advice:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Shower as standard.</strong> Most guests want a good, spacious shower with a strong flow. A walk-in shower with a thermostatic mixer scores well in reviews and can be cleaned in minutes.</li>
    <li><strong>Rain shower plus hand shower</strong> gives the luxury feel guests photograph, while the hand shower keeps cleaning easy.</li>
    <li><strong>One bath for families.</strong> Parents with small children search for ‘bath’. If you have a family room or a family holiday home, keep a shower bath there.</li>
    <li><strong>Freestanding bath in one suite</strong> if you want to offer a premium room. It is a strong image for the website, but costs water, space and cleaning time.</li>
    <li><strong>Robust and easy to replace:</strong> choose standard sizes and Spanish brands, so a broken tap or screen can be replaced quickly.</li>
    <li><strong>Extraction:</strong> many bathrooms in village houses have no window. A good extractor fan with a timer prevents mould between changeovers. See <a href="/en/problems/damp-and-mould/">damp and mould</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Buying or starting a B&B? Also read <a href="/en/guides/finca-apartment-or-village-house/bed-and-breakfast/">which property suits a B&B</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="later">A bathroom for later life</h2>
  <p>Many owners in the Axarquía live there after retiring. If you build a new bathroom now, think ahead: a step-free walk-in shower, a slip-resistant floor, a thermostatic mixer against scalding, reinforcement in the wall so grab rails can be added later, and room for a shower seat. It costs next to nothing extra now and avoids a second renovation.</p>

